WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.628
[MUSIC]

00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:04.838
How you show up matters.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.391
Having a powerful presence can make or

00:00:07.391 --> 00:00:11.957
break your transactions,
your relationships, and your career.

00:00:11.957 --> 00:00:13.805
Hello, I'm Matt Abrahams, and

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:18.035
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:18.035 --> 00:00:22.543
Welcome to Think Fast,
Talk Smart, the Podcast.

00:00:22.543 --> 00:00:26.115
Today I'm really excited to
be joined by Jeff Pfeffer,

00:00:26.115 --> 00:00:30.452
who is a professor of
Organizational Behavior at Stanford's GSB.

00:00:30.452 --> 00:00:34.453
Jeff's research focuses on the effects
of work environments on health and

00:00:34.453 --> 00:00:38.017
well-being, as well as power and
leadership in organizations.

00:00:38.017 --> 00:00:42.067
He teaches the super popular
course The Paths to Power, and

00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:45.550
he is the author of many books,
including Dying For

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:50.172
A Paycheck, and Power,
Why Some People Have It and Others Don't.

00:00:50.172 --> 00:00:52.714
Welcome, Jeff, thanks for being here.

00:00:52.714 --> 00:00:55.861
&gt;&gt; Well, Matt, thank you for
having me on your show.

00:00:55.861 --> 00:00:58.926
&gt;&gt; Great,
I'm super excited to get started.

00:00:58.926 --> 00:01:03.465
So my first question is how do
you define power and status, and

00:01:03.465 --> 00:01:08.366
why do you think your class that
covers these topics is so popular?

00:01:08.366 --> 00:01:14.516
&gt;&gt; I define power as the ability to
get things done against opposition.

00:01:14.516 --> 00:01:17.493
And oftentimes, of course,
as we try to make things happen,

00:01:17.493 --> 00:01:19.568
we find that not everybody agrees with us.

00:01:19.568 --> 00:01:22.259
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I think the class is popular

00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:25.585
because I have this very bad
habit of teaching people and

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.374
actually telling the truth.

00:01:27.374 --> 00:01:31.462
And so therefore, I think my students,
both online and offline,

00:01:31.462 --> 00:01:36.208
and everybody I talk to, actually
appreciates knowing how to get things done

00:01:36.208 --> 00:01:39.363
in a world in which
oftentimes that's difficult.

00:01:39.363 --> 00:01:41.509
And they like hearing the truth.

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:45.455
As I like to say,
Jack Nicholson was actually wrong,

00:01:45.455 --> 00:01:47.835
people do want to hear the truth.

00:01:47.835 --> 00:01:51.181
&gt;&gt; That's one of my favorite movies,
for sure, and

00:01:51.181 --> 00:01:55.813
I chuckled when you said often those
in power can't get things done.

00:01:55.813 --> 00:02:00.589
As the father of two teenagers
that is certainly true.

00:02:00.589 --> 00:02:01.732
&gt;&gt; It's true in companies as well.

00:02:01.732 --> 00:02:03.648
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes, yes.

00:02:03.648 --> 00:02:07.621
For my next few questions,
I want to get very tactical and specific,

00:02:07.621 --> 00:02:08.817
if you don't mind.

00:02:08.817 --> 00:02:14.063
So are there specific nonverbal
behaviors or body language, if you will,

00:02:14.063 --> 00:02:19.075
that we can employ to be seen as more
powerful and having higher status?

00:02:19.075 --> 00:02:22.515
Are there things we should
avoid doing as well?

00:02:22.515 --> 00:02:28.293
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and in fact, I'm sure
this is what you teach in your class.

00:02:28.293 --> 00:02:31.374
We give away our power
by tilting our heads,

00:02:31.374 --> 00:02:34.131
we give away our power by looking down.

00:02:34.131 --> 00:02:39.204
We give away our power particularly
when we hunch over and

00:02:39.204 --> 00:02:42.381
we constrict our body in on itself.

00:02:42.381 --> 00:02:44.957
If you want to see an example of this,

00:02:44.957 --> 00:02:49.697
watch Tony Hayward testifying in
the BP hearings, where he looks,

00:02:49.697 --> 00:02:54.704
there's no gestures and he's really
hunched in and reading a script.

00:02:54.704 --> 00:02:59.332
And so reading a statement, and so all
of those things I think give away power.

00:02:59.332 --> 00:03:04.906
And on the contrary, you have power to the
extent that you look people in the eye.

00:03:04.906 --> 00:03:08.316
You have power to the extent that
you stand up to your full height,

00:03:08.316 --> 00:03:09.319
whatever that is.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:15.290
You have power to the extent that
you take an expansive posture.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.519
You spread out,
you take up more space, and

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:21.148
you certainly have power to the extent
that you use forceful gestures.

00:03:21.148 --> 00:03:25.658
And anybody who's ever watched
somebody speak, or watch the movie

00:03:25.658 --> 00:03:30.419
understands what a forceful gesture
is as opposed to a one that isn't.

00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:33.697
&gt;&gt; Excellent, so you're right,
in the classes I teach,

00:03:33.697 --> 00:03:38.040
we talk about being big, balanced,
and still to demonstrate presence.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.059
And it sounds like you're
echoing that advice.

00:03:41.059 --> 00:03:41.912
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:03:41.912 --> 00:03:44.993
&gt;&gt; Let's switch from nonverbal to verbal.

00:03:44.993 --> 00:03:49.780
Are there specific verbal behaviors we
can employ to be seen as more powerful or

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:51.242
having higher status?

00:03:51.242 --> 00:03:54.626
And are there things that
we should avoid saying?

00:03:54.626 --> 00:03:55.476
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:03:55.476 --> 00:03:59.500
one of the ways in which you
convey power is by being loud.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.527
One of the ways in which you convey
power is by being modestly or

00:04:03.527 --> 00:04:07.099
moderately impolite and
interrupting other people.

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:12.236
And by contrast, one way in which
you don't give away your power,

00:04:12.236 --> 00:04:15.371
if you refuse yourself be interrupted.

00:04:15.371 --> 00:04:19.195
So that's one set of things.

00:04:19.195 --> 00:04:21.066
And then there's powerful language.

00:04:21.066 --> 00:04:26.106
You want to use simple language,
forceful language,

00:04:26.106 --> 00:04:32.053
vivid language, vivid words,
and keep it simple and direct.

00:04:32.053 --> 00:04:36.206
&gt;&gt; Very, very direct and
simple in your answer, thank you.

00:04:36.206 --> 00:04:41.134
I'm curious your thoughts, because I deal
a lot with this in the work that I do,

00:04:41.134 --> 00:04:45.570
around filler words and words of
hesitation, like kind of and sort of.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:50.234
Do you have insights on that and
how that affects how people perceive us?

00:04:50.234 --> 00:04:53.684
&gt;&gt; You don't want to use filler words and
you don't want to use to the extent

00:04:53.684 --> 00:04:56.273
possible um's or ah's or
anything else like that.

00:04:56.273 --> 00:05:00.748
You want to speak as if,
even though it's not rehearsed, it's been

00:05:00.748 --> 00:05:05.626
completely rehearsed and you want to
not have any filler words whatsoever.

00:05:05.626 --> 00:05:08.522
&gt;&gt; Okay, so fluency is important for sure.

00:05:08.522 --> 00:05:10.342
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:05:10.342 --> 00:05:13.634
&gt;&gt; Let's move away from verbal and
nonverbal behavior, and

00:05:13.634 --> 00:05:18.218
I'm curious if you have any thoughts on
how emotion relates to power and status.

00:05:18.218 --> 00:05:23.026
Are there certain emotions we may want
to display while avoiding others?

00:05:23.026 --> 00:05:28.602
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, but in general I think we
want to see people come across strong.

00:05:28.602 --> 00:05:33.060
Anger is a much stronger
emotion than sadness or

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.972
than any kind of remorse or
hesitation or whatever.

00:05:37.972 --> 00:05:40.629
So anger's a very strong emotion, and

00:05:40.629 --> 00:05:44.989
I have found in dealing with
the various people that I deal with.

00:05:44.989 --> 00:05:48.096
And particularly the institutions
that I deal with,

00:05:48.096 --> 00:05:52.665
many of which have done a very good job
of not providing good customer service-

00:05:52.665 --> 00:05:53.715
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:53.715 --> 00:05:56.117
&gt;&gt; And your audience can figure out who

00:05:56.117 --> 00:06:01.724
they are, that oftentimes displaying anger
gets much more results than begging or

00:06:01.724 --> 00:06:03.343
cajoling or pleading.

00:06:03.343 --> 00:06:06.495
Or throwing yourself on the mercy of

00:06:06.495 --> 00:06:11.333
whoever the particular
organizations happens to be.

00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:16.260
Then you should use strong emotions,
and anger and forcefulness is much

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:20.635
stronger than pleading, or
apology, or something like that.

00:06:20.635 --> 00:06:24.824
&gt;&gt; So there are certain situations for
sure where anger is appropriate,

00:06:24.824 --> 00:06:26.814
there are others where it is not.

00:06:26.814 --> 00:06:31.873
Is something just like being very
passionate and convicted in your belief,

00:06:31.873 --> 00:06:35.124
or what you're saying,
does that help as well?

00:06:35.124 --> 00:06:40.038
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, there's no question that
to the extent that you are excited,

00:06:40.038 --> 00:06:43.131
I mean,
we know that emotions are contagious.

00:06:43.131 --> 00:06:46.414
You walk down an airline corridor to the
extent that anybody walks down an airline

00:06:46.414 --> 00:06:47.245
corridor anymore.

00:06:47.245 --> 00:06:50.491
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Smile at people, they smile back,

00:06:50.491 --> 00:06:54.973
if you frown, they'll frown, so
we know that there have been studies.

00:06:54.973 --> 00:06:59.116
Emotions are contagious, and
that means energy is contagious,

00:06:59.116 --> 00:07:02.513
passion is contagious,
conviction is contagious.

00:07:02.513 --> 00:07:06.086
So yes, you want to be passionate,
you want to be energetic,

00:07:06.086 --> 00:07:09.753
you want to have conviction
about what you're talking about.

00:07:09.753 --> 00:07:14.189
Confidence is contagious, you want to
display confidence both in the words and

00:07:14.189 --> 00:07:16.982
in body language and
in the language that you use.

00:07:16.982 --> 00:07:20.084
You don't want to say I think
there may be some chance

00:07:20.084 --> 00:07:22.330
that our new venture might succeed.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.119
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You want to say we are going to succeed

00:07:25.119 --> 00:07:27.027
and there's no question about it.

00:07:27.027 --> 00:07:31.555
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Great example, I can't tell you
the number of entrepreneurs I work with

00:07:31.555 --> 00:07:34.383
who have such hedging
language in what they say.

00:07:34.383 --> 00:07:38.665
Yet they are completely involved and
engaged in what they're saying and

00:07:38.665 --> 00:07:40.007
passionate about it.

00:07:40.007 --> 00:07:44.852
It strikes me, Jeff, that what you just
talked about in terms of strong emotions,

00:07:44.852 --> 00:07:49.147
that actually leads to some of the things
you discussed with nonverbal and

00:07:49.147 --> 00:07:50.271
verbal behavior.

00:07:50.271 --> 00:07:54.572
When we are passionate about something or
angry about something, we gesture broadly,

00:07:54.572 --> 00:07:57.838
we're more expressive in our voice,
the language choice we use.

00:07:57.838 --> 00:08:01.260
So the emotion can actually
drive some of that nonverbal and

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.181
verbal behavior you mentioned.

00:08:03.181 --> 00:08:04.708
&gt;&gt; No, that's exactly correct.

00:08:04.708 --> 00:08:09.264
I mean, to the extent that you're
passionate and convicted and confident,

00:08:09.264 --> 00:08:12.079
you're more likely to
speak in a louder voice.

00:08:12.079 --> 00:08:16.925
&gt;&gt; Right, right, right, so you were very
kind to give me a sneak peek at your

00:08:16.925 --> 00:08:20.852
upcoming book, and
I'm very excited for that to come out.

00:08:20.852 --> 00:08:25.531
In it, you talk about how our faces
convey a lot of useful information

00:08:25.531 --> 00:08:27.802
regarding our power and status.

00:08:27.802 --> 00:08:32.464
Given that we are spending a lot
of time communicating virtually

00:08:32.464 --> 00:08:34.632
where only our faces show up.

00:08:34.632 --> 00:08:39.405
What advice would you give to people who
want to be perceived as competent in

00:08:39.405 --> 00:08:43.977
higher status, when all we see is
a video representation of our faces?

00:08:43.977 --> 00:08:47.952
&gt;&gt; Well I think, in the picture I'm
looking at of you now I think is

00:08:47.952 --> 00:08:50.525
a fabulous example of-
&gt;&gt; Why, thank you.

00:08:50.525 --> 00:08:52.565
&gt;&gt; So you practice what you teach.

00:08:52.565 --> 00:08:56.853
I think people,
even prior to the pandemic, but

00:08:56.853 --> 00:09:01.771
certainly since the pandemic
have gotten way too sloppy

00:09:01.771 --> 00:09:05.745
about how they dress and
how they show up, and

00:09:05.745 --> 00:09:10.683
I mean maybe Mark Zuckerberg
can get away with a hoodie.

00:09:10.683 --> 00:09:12.802
I think for most people you can't.

00:09:12.802 --> 00:09:15.999
I think you should dress and

00:09:15.999 --> 00:09:21.426
show up as how you want
people to perceive you.

00:09:21.426 --> 00:09:25.090
&gt;&gt; So first and foremost,
the folks behind my style,

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:27.742
if I have any, will be very pleased.

00:09:27.742 --> 00:09:30.081
My wife and
my two kids are going to be very thankful,

00:09:30.081 --> 00:09:32.936
because I could not do anything,
so thank you for noticing that.

00:09:32.936 --> 00:09:37.385
And then second, it really sounds just
like we need to be thinking about what

00:09:37.385 --> 00:09:41.571
we say in our messages and how we
structure them for presence and power.

00:09:41.571 --> 00:09:45.530
We also need to think about how we
show up, and some of the literature

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:49.708
that I'm familiar with suggests that
how you frame yourself matters.

00:09:49.708 --> 00:09:53.034
You should take up half the screen and
not sitting too far away.

00:09:53.034 --> 00:09:57.097
We read a lot on people's faces, and if I
can't see your face because you're too far

00:09:57.097 --> 00:10:01.406
away or you're sitting in the dark, that
actually makes you look more suspicious,

00:10:01.406 --> 00:10:02.272
less powerful.

00:10:02.272 --> 00:10:06.831
So it's not only how you dress and
how you look, as you mentioned,

00:10:06.831 --> 00:10:10.181
it's also how you frame
yourself also matters.

00:10:10.181 --> 00:10:13.057
Let me ask you this question.

00:10:13.057 --> 00:10:17.456
What would you suggest that someone
do who is in a lower status or

00:10:17.456 --> 00:10:22.188
power position, who wants to make
sure someone in a higher status or

00:10:22.188 --> 00:10:26.359
power position,
pays attention to their communication?

00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:30.495
For example, a line employee
talking to his or her boss, or

00:10:30.495 --> 00:10:32.581
even me talking to our deans?

00:10:32.581 --> 00:10:37.314
&gt;&gt; I think you need to think hard
about what you're going to say,

00:10:37.314 --> 00:10:39.772
and you want to make it concise,

00:10:39.772 --> 00:10:44.710
and you want to lead with the most
powerful part of your message.

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:47.902
And the part of the message you want
them to pay the most attention to,

00:10:47.902 --> 00:10:50.505
because people have always
had short attention spans.

00:10:50.505 --> 00:10:52.939
And the attention spans have gone down,

00:10:52.939 --> 00:10:56.887
I think you want to do pretty much
what we've been talking about.

00:10:56.887 --> 00:11:01.038
You want to speak directly, you want to
communicate directly, you want to show up,

00:11:01.038 --> 00:11:03.491
if you're communicating
with them in person.

00:11:03.491 --> 00:11:09.132
In a way that shows that you're
someone to be taken seriously,

00:11:09.132 --> 00:11:13.294
and in how you dress and
how you hold yourself.

00:11:13.294 --> 00:11:16.527
And how you convey yourself through
both your body language and

00:11:16.527 --> 00:11:17.754
your spoken language.

00:11:17.754 --> 00:11:22.480
So I think you would do all the things
that we've talked about regardless of who

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:25.998
you're talking to,
if you want your message to be heard.

00:11:25.998 --> 00:11:30.280
&gt;&gt; Excellent advice,
I often add in these situations to somehow

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:35.153
link what you're saying to something
of value to them can be helpful.

00:11:35.153 --> 00:11:39.317
Not only to demonstrate that you
understand what's important for them, but

00:11:39.317 --> 00:11:43.750
that you've taken the time to make your
message relevant and tailored it as well.

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:48.483
&gt;&gt; Yes, well, if you want to tailor your
message, I mean, you should always as we

00:11:48.483 --> 00:11:52.717
understand, you should begin,
of course, by flattering them, and

00:11:52.717 --> 00:11:57.277
the research suggests that there is no
amount of flattery that is too much.

00:11:57.277 --> 00:12:01.329
So you want to tell them how much
you admire what they've done and

00:12:01.329 --> 00:12:05.321
how much you respect their abilities and
all this other stuff.

00:12:05.321 --> 00:12:10.559
If you want them to take you seriously,
you want to show them how much you admire

00:12:10.559 --> 00:12:16.057
them and how much you respect them, and
how happy you are to be in their presence.

00:12:16.057 --> 00:12:20.978
&gt;&gt; Well, Jeff, that is probably the best
answer I've ever had on this podcast,

00:12:20.978 --> 00:12:25.540
and I so admire you taking the time to
actually address that answer to us.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:30.713
I'm curious if you have,
upon reflecting on your research and

00:12:30.713 --> 00:12:35.593
your experience,
any last bits of advice around how to have

00:12:35.593 --> 00:12:40.494
a powerful high status presence
that you'd like to share?

00:12:40.494 --> 00:12:44.720
&gt;&gt; Well, I think when I have
Bill English and Susi Damilano,

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:49.294
who are co-founders of
the San Francisco Playhouse, come and

00:12:49.294 --> 00:12:52.156
teach my session on acting with power.

00:12:52.156 --> 00:12:55.660
They give the students I
think fabulous advice,

00:12:55.660 --> 00:13:01.225
which I will pass on on their behalf to
your listeners, and that is to warm up.

00:13:01.225 --> 00:13:06.998
So they talk about getting your voice
warmed up, getting your body warmed up,

00:13:06.998 --> 00:13:11.284
obviously not in public,
maybe shaking yourself out, or

00:13:11.284 --> 00:13:15.322
shaking your body or
going [SOUND] to warm up your voice.

00:13:15.322 --> 00:13:18.988
So that when you are, I mean,
you can see actors and

00:13:18.988 --> 00:13:24.188
actresses do this before they go on
stage or before they go to get filmed,

00:13:24.188 --> 00:13:28.135
but you should warm up in
advance of everything you do.

00:13:28.135 --> 00:13:32.195
And one of the things you should do as
part of the warm-up process is calm

00:13:32.195 --> 00:13:33.151
yourself down.

00:13:33.151 --> 00:13:36.518
So that you don't show
up in a nervous fashion,

00:13:36.518 --> 00:13:39.222
and you show up basically ready to go.

00:13:39.222 --> 00:13:43.320
I mean, if I said to you, Matt,
you're going to go play football,

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:48.238
particularly given our ages, but even
before you went on the football field,

00:13:48.238 --> 00:13:52.506
you would probably do some stretches and
some exercises to warm up.

00:13:52.506 --> 00:13:58.597
And the same advice holds for speech and
communication, you want to warm up.

00:13:58.597 --> 00:14:02.038
So that when you enter the moment,

00:14:02.038 --> 00:14:06.912
you are as relaxed and
as ready to go as you can be.

00:14:06.912 --> 00:14:10.391
&gt;&gt; If I were playing football,
Jeff, I would sign my will and

00:14:10.391 --> 00:14:12.956
power of attorney and
then I would warm up.

00:14:12.956 --> 00:14:17.932
But, [LAUGH] you're exactly right
in terms of taking time to warm up.

00:14:17.932 --> 00:14:22.668
So many people are so focused on what
they're saying they don't take time to

00:14:22.668 --> 00:14:27.926
actually prepare their bodies, their
minds, their voice for actually saying it.

00:14:27.926 --> 00:14:31.871
And we often are so busy in rushing around
that we don't give ourselves even time to

00:14:31.871 --> 00:14:33.712
prepare the content as best we could.

00:14:33.712 --> 00:14:39.763
So that's wonderful advice to really
build into the process a time to warm up.

00:14:39.763 --> 00:14:44.408
And the great thing about being virtual is
nobody needs to see it until you turn your

00:14:44.408 --> 00:14:48.122
camera on and unmute your mic,
you can do some of that warming up.

00:14:48.122 --> 00:14:52.105
So there are actually some
advantages I think to being virtual.

00:14:52.105 --> 00:14:57.002
Now, before we end, I'd like to ask
you the same three questions I ask

00:14:57.002 --> 00:15:00.079
everyone who joins me,
are you up for that?

00:15:00.079 --> 00:15:01.058
&gt;&gt; I'm up for that.

00:15:01.058 --> 00:15:03.251
&gt;&gt; Okay, [LAUGH] he says hesitantly.

00:15:03.251 --> 00:15:05.728
&gt;&gt; I'm not sure I have a choice but
sure, of course.

00:15:05.728 --> 00:15:08.996
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yes,
I have the power in this situation.

00:15:08.996 --> 00:15:13.193
If you were to capture the best
communication advice you have

00:15:13.193 --> 00:15:17.969
ever received as a five-to-seven
word presentation slide title,

00:15:17.969 --> 00:15:19.216
what would it be?

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:24.164
&gt;&gt; Warm up, don't be nervous.

00:15:24.164 --> 00:15:29.486
&gt;&gt; I like it, very, very concise, and
I think with the exception of one word,

00:15:29.486 --> 00:15:34.161
they all were one-syllable words,
warm up, don't be nervous.

00:15:34.161 --> 00:15:37.968
And we have spent time on this podcast
definitely talking about anxiety

00:15:37.968 --> 00:15:38.733
management.

00:15:38.733 --> 00:15:41.348
So those who are still nervous speaking,

00:15:41.348 --> 00:15:44.346
I encourage you to listen
in on those episodes.

00:15:44.346 --> 00:15:48.621
Number two, Jeff, who is a communicator
that you admire, and why?

00:15:53.105 --> 00:15:57.165
&gt;&gt; I'm going to back up and
ask you to define admire.

00:15:57.165 --> 00:15:57.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:02.521
most people take this question to
mean somebody that they respect.

00:16:02.521 --> 00:16:07.353
Somebody that they feel demonstrates
communication skills that

00:16:07.353 --> 00:16:11.670
are worthy of others to consider and
use, does that help?

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.296
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that helps,
I'm going to give you two answers.

00:16:14.296 --> 00:16:15.136
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:16:15.136 --> 00:16:19.053
&gt;&gt; I think Barack Obama is
a fabulous communicator, but

00:16:19.053 --> 00:16:24.026
I also think Donald Trump is
an extremely effective communicator.

00:16:24.026 --> 00:16:28.355
And I think people can learn a lot
by watching what he does and

00:16:28.355 --> 00:16:32.080
how he manages an audience,
and how he responds and

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:36.514
feeds off the audience, and
how he plays into the audience.

00:16:36.514 --> 00:16:41.214
And how what you say doesn't matter
as much as how you say it, and

00:16:41.214 --> 00:16:44.715
there's a lot of research
that suggests that.

00:16:44.715 --> 00:16:49.595
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, and a lot of people
have looked at his speaking style and

00:16:49.595 --> 00:16:52.579
identified things that
we can all learn from.

00:16:52.579 --> 00:16:57.334
So number three, the final question,
what are the first three

00:16:57.334 --> 00:17:02.101
ingredients that go into
a successful communication recipe?

00:17:02.101 --> 00:17:04.503
&gt;&gt; A successful communication,
I didn't hear the last word.

00:17:04.503 --> 00:17:08.705
&gt;&gt; Recipe, imagine you're a cook in
a kitchen putting together a successful

00:17:08.705 --> 00:17:12.190
communication dish,
what are the first three ingredients?

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:17.294
&gt;&gt; Simplicity, simplicity for sure,

00:17:17.294 --> 00:17:22.892
forcefulness, and third ingredient,

00:17:22.892 --> 00:17:28.994
simplicity, forcefulness, and volume.

00:17:28.994 --> 00:17:33.003
&gt;&gt; Interesting, tell me a little
bit more about forcefulness.

00:17:33.003 --> 00:17:36.370
I think we get simplicity and
I think volume makes sense,

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:38.623
but what do you mean by forcefulness?

00:17:38.623 --> 00:17:43.857
&gt;&gt; Forcefulness means
speaking with conviction.

00:17:43.857 --> 00:17:47.293
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Which means not only your voice and

00:17:47.293 --> 00:17:53.870
not only the loudness of your voice,
but acting as if you mean what you say.

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:59.943
And there's this old saying, which I think
has been taken very badly out of context.

00:17:59.943 --> 00:18:04.568
And which I think has been misused in some
sense, which is fake it till you make it.

00:18:04.568 --> 00:18:08.613
I think the real saying is
fake it until you become it.

00:18:08.613 --> 00:18:12.856
If you say something often enough and with
enough conviction over time, you may not

00:18:12.856 --> 00:18:16.744
convince any other human beings but
you will certainly convince yourself.

00:18:16.744 --> 00:18:20.581
And for you to be able to convince
anybody else of something,

00:18:20.581 --> 00:18:23.976
you have to believe what's
coming out of your mouth.

00:18:23.976 --> 00:18:27.283
&gt;&gt; That's a great way of explaining
forcefulness, thank you.

00:18:27.283 --> 00:18:30.371
And thank you, Jeff, for
taking time to be with us today.

00:18:30.371 --> 00:18:34.769
All of us are truly appreciative of
the detailed specific advice and

00:18:34.769 --> 00:18:37.020
guidance that you have provided.

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:40.938
And I expect that all of us will
be sitting up a little straighter,

00:18:40.938 --> 00:18:42.515
talking a little louder.

00:18:42.515 --> 00:18:44.525
Being a little forceful, so

00:18:44.525 --> 00:18:49.813
that we too can have a strong presence
that's powerful and high in status.

00:18:49.813 --> 00:18:51.099
Thanks so much, Jeff.

00:18:51.099 --> 00:18:52.063
